  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "As with any propaganda, I would hope that it would lead people to think twice about whether to forward it themselves. I hope it will lead people to think twice about what they're seeing on the internet. I get middle school kids come to my office all the time, high school kids. I see them in Colorado as well. And I often ask them, do you write papers in your classes? And if the answer is no, then we have a whole different conversation. But if the answer is yes, which it typically is, I then say, well, do you have to rely on the internet to do your research? And they say, yes, we do. And I said, do you have to distinguish when you're doing your research between curated facts? And we talk about what that means, edited content. or somebody just shooting their mouth off on the internet. And they said, of course we do. Why? Because our teachers will flunk us if we don't. And for years, my plea for Congress has been that we subscribe to a middle class standard or middle school standard of factual analysis when it comes to taking stuff off the internet. And now there's a whole other layer of this. Because before 2016, we didn't know that the Russians and now others are using it for propaganda purposes. And I think it's really important for the Americans to be able to see it, understand it, identify it, or at least ask questions about it. But I also hope in the long run it will build public support for the legislation that we need to pass to protect our democracy. And I hope that it will build pressure on the social media companies to do a better job of policing themselves in the absence of what may become statutory and regulatory efforts to contend with the effect of this kind of propaganda on our politics and on our discourse through the use of those platforms.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
