  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "You noted that the Russians are picking up on narratives that existed before they began their influence operations on social media. Pre-existing narratives, a dialogue that looks indistinguishable in many instances from Americans. And in fact, what our research has shown is that they are increasingly just actually picking up authentic dialogue among Americans and expanding that and spreading that and amplifying that. when it serves their purpose, when it is the kind of divisive rhetoric that they are seeking to promote, which I think does make it all that more challenging to figure out whether it's from the social media platforms or otherwise how to address that. How does that affect the way you think about what social media companies should be doing to address this?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Russians are picking up on pre-existing narratives that existed before their influence operations.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "These pre-existing narratives often resemble American dialogue.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Our research shows that Russian actors increasingly adopt authentic American dialogue.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Russian actors expand, spread, and amplify selected American dialogues.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "They do so primarily when the discourse is divisive and aligns with their objectives.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "This makes it difficult to distinguish between genuine and manipulated discourse online.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "It complicates efforts to determine the origin of harmful content on social media platforms.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Social media platforms have a responsibility to monitor and mitigate foreign interference.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Authentic public discourse within the U.S. population can be exploited by foreign actors for strategic purposes.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Divisive rhetoric is a primary tool used by Russia to destabilize democratic processes.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a clear distinction between organic and coordinated disinformation campaigns, even if it's hard to detect.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Research findings from the speaker’s team are credible and representative of broader trends.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Audiences are vulnerable to manipulation through seemingly authentic conversations.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Addressing misinformation requires distinguishing source origins, especially when they mimic domestic speech.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 10,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think they need to do a lot more. Facebook didn't even, I mean, they sort of laughed it off to begin with. They didn't admit it's the wrong word, but they denied that this was the Russians. They denied that the Russians have paid them in rubles to buy these ads. And then when you raise issues about it with them, what they'll say is, well, we're just a platform. It's not our job to police. And I believe that when I think about, I'm old now, I'm 54 years old, so when I talk about this stuff, I sound like I'm well past my cell date. I don't believe it's acceptable for American companies that are as powerful as these companies are to take the position that they're just mere platforms and that there's nothing they can do about the corrosiveness on social media. at the last ten years that I've been in the Senate, and that's been corresponded with the rise in social media. It's not obvious to me that social media companies couldn't have ended up being a force for democratic good in this world and in this country. have given people that were marginalized the opportunity to participate in the democracy in a more profound way than they were before. But that hasn't been the story for the last 10 years, and hopefully it will evolve. It's been very corrosive, and we see the stories just in the last couple weeks about the ways in which the Chinese are using this, and it's turned out that the tools have been much more effectively used by tyrants than they have people in the streets and by the Russians in our case. So I do think they've got a responsibility to police themselves and if they don't do it, we're going to police them in some fashion. And I think we should. The First Amendment isn't really implicated here because we're not talking about the federal government. Obviously we have values of free expression that we cherish. But I, for one, in all aspects of our society would like to see a less degraded democracy, a less degraded conversation, the one that we're having with each other as Americans. I take it as a responsibility as an elected official to take the incoming that I get on Facebook. It's not a pretty picture. And I doubt very much whether we're going to solve the problems that our kids and grandkids need us to solve along the lines of the discourse that's happening on my Facebook page or anybody else's Facebook page, but it doesn't have to stay that way. And how they respond to this propaganda and help us protect ourselves going forward into the future. I think we'll say a lot about the role they think they can play that's constructive in our democracy and our society. And I'm optimistic. I think they're learning from what happened and I think things will get better.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
