  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "We're having that debate as a country right now. And the more I think about it, the more I believe that this place that we are all so lucky to inhabit, from our very founding, we have had the most awesome impulses of humankind and some of the worst impulses of humankind. And it's been sort of a dialectic over 230 years about which impulse is going to emerged and we're in one of those moments right now. When you think about the founding of this country, I've tried to remind crowds of this these days, the founders did not believe that we would agree with each other. That was not the proposition of why we were going to live in a free republic, in a democratic republic. Their idea was not that we would agree with each other, but that we would disagree with each other. And out of those disagreements, we would create more imaginative and more durable solutions than any king or tyrant could come up with on their own. That was the theory of the case. And so the discourse and the debate is a really important part of that. And coming to a result is a really important part of that, as my friend Lamar Alexander from Tennessee likes to say, if you don't want to come to a result, there's no reason for you to come to Congress, you might as well just stay home and be on the radio if what you want to do is just have your point of view. So I think it's very true that what they were trying to do was exploit our worst impulses, not our best impulses, and we're vulnerable to that as a society. And I believe that our pluralism actually is an enormous strength. in America, and that the more diverse, as you were saying, the voices are in our political decision-making, the stronger we are as a country, and I know not everybody agrees with that and believes that. I do believe that, and I think we'll prove it on the other side of this presidency.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The United States is currently engaged in a national debate.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "American history has reflected both great human impulses and terrible ones over the past 230 years.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The Founding Fathers expected disagreement rather than consensus among citizens.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The American system is based on a dialectical tension between positive and negative impulses.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Pluralism in American political life is a significant strength.",
