  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "It's critically important, it's critically important, not just so that people can understand how they can have an influence over the mechanisms of government and what they can do and the other citizens or potential citizens they can register to vote, all those things are true, but also to learn our own history. That is so vital. People have a tendency to believe When they wander around Washington, the same kids come to see me, that this was all just here. of it was just here. The Supreme Court wasn't just here. The fact that my daughters, all three of them, have the right to vote wasn't just here. The fact that we ended human slavery, we started human slavery and perpetuated human slavery in this country and then ended it, wasn't always here. It was Americans fulfilling their role as citizens and in my other book. the land of flickering lights, I contend that what that role really is is the role of founder. We have to think of ourselves as founders of this Republic every single minute. It's that elevated a responsibility that we have as citizens in this Republic. And this, I don't mean this in partisan terms, it is the opposite impulse of I alone can fix it, which is what the President of the United States asserted when he ran for office the last time, I think. that it's very important, obviously, who is living at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue to our future. But I think what's more important than that is what role we're playing as citizens, including kids that are learning for the first time about our democracy and about our republic. I'm sorry to go on for so long, but one other thought. I was with my middle daughter. last month at the African-American History Museum here in Washington. I hadn't been there before, and we were in the first two floors. And one of the things I was thinking about as I was walking through with her was how visible all the images were to me, how familiar they were to me of the Civil Rights Movement. four years old in 1968 when MLK was killed, but in the early 70s, this was current, all of it was, and it's not in the same way to the next generation or the generation after that, and that reminds us what responsibility we all have to transmit our own history to people so that they realize, because it's never given here in a democracy, it's always something that the American people are able to build on their, build through force of their will in an organization and through that spirited debate. It doesn't change, doesn't happen any other way, whether it's guns or healthcare or anything like that.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Supreme Court did not exist from the beginning of the nation.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "It is critically important for people to understand how they can influence government mechanisms.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Human slavery existed in America and was later abolished.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Learning American history is vital for citizenship.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Citizenship involves actively shaping the Republic rather than passively inheriting it.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Transmitting history to younger generations is crucial for sustaining democracy.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "My daughters have the right to vote due to historical developments, not inherent conditions.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Every citizen should view themselves as a founder of the Republic.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Registering others to vote is an essential civic action.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
