  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "I quote at the beginning and end of my book, James Baldwin's incredible essay, The Fire Next Time, that he wrote in the New Yorker at the crisis years of the civil rights movement. And what he writes in that incredible essay is, everything now is in our hands. We have no right to assume otherwise. which I think captures perfectly what our obligations are as citizens. We have no right to assume otherwise, because if we do assume otherwise, what needs to be done, that would be done. And I happen to know I've spent a lot of time with young people, and I know they are deeply concerned that we are not doing what we're supposed to be doing to put them in a position where they can do what they need to do, whether it's on the economy or climate or America's role in the world. They're just worried about the position we're going to leave them in.",
