    "turn_text": "app you could pick. No, that's when it's particularly my favorite app. Somebody told me once, they said, Michael, when you hire a pollster, You have to believe your pollster. And if you don't believe your pollster and your politician, you should get rid of your pollster. But if you keep your pollster, you have to believe them. You can't simultaneously hold on your pollster and doubt what your pollster is reporting to you. That's how I feel about ways, exactly how I feel about ways, which is I've given myself up to ways, which means that when I'm trying to get here at 5.30, and I now know I'll be here at 5.32, and there's nothing I can do about it, I have no stress. Whereas two years ago, I would have been a complete mess on the way over here. But I have not, in my mind, contracted with Google, as notwithstanding how much I love ways, uh... to allow them to know everywhere i've been or where i'm going you know this evening and they're taking the view that i have contracted with them might my view is that if i have it's a contract of adhesion not one that actually negotiate with them and i believe we are about to have a really interesting debate in america and in the congress about the issues that you raise and it is going to be absolutely fascinating because So much of it is good. I mean, my kid's view of privacy is very different from my view of privacy. It's very different from my view of what their view of privacy should be as they think about their interaction with the internet. We are going to move into a new era, I think, where the guiding principle, which has been, you have no right to complain as long as what you're getting is free, is going to turn out not to be the guiding principle anymore. And people, it's not just going to be about private privacy issues. It's going to be about who gets the benefit of monetizing these issues, this data, who gets the benefit of the economics. And we are in a very early phase on this, but I can tell you there are people on both sides of the aisle who are thinking hard about the privacy question that you raised.",
