  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "On the first question, I don't have a reaction except that it's more chaos at the White House. And when you've had more national security advisers than you've had years in the White House, it suggests why we may seem to lack a strategy when it comes to much of anything, whether it's China or Russia or the Middle East. So that's the extent of my reaction to that. So the second question. I thought it was a terrible decision the President made to abandon the Iran deal. I never thought the Iran deal was perfect. I had concerns about the limitation of time. I had concerns about its scope. When I voted for it, there was a lot that was unknown about what Iran's compliance with the deal would look like. And those unknowns were known by the time President Trump became president. And what was known was that the Iranians had been pushed back in terms of the breakout to nuclear weapon by almost a year, for they've gone from two to three months to a year, which is really vital because when it's two or three months, it's very hard to mobilize allies in that frame of time to be able to react. When you have a year, you have the time to be able to do it. And I think, very unfortunately, we've now been put in a position of putting the Iranians in the position to decide how provocative they want to be and where they want to draw the lines. And they are not surprisingly being provocative. I always believe that the conventional threat they posed to Israel and the conventional threat that they posed to the region and the US would be far worse backstop by the possibility of their acquiring a nuclear weapon than not acquiring a nuclear weapon. And now we find ourselves in the position of their beginning to enrich again. We've also to see this daylight between ourselves and our allies who negotiated this deal. I don't think any of that is good for the United States and for our strategic interest in the region. And I'll get back to you on the other two questions. I want to give you a considered answer.",
