  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I do. In the last point, I think absolutely. And even before you get to the privacy questions, there's no reason why foreign actors and foreign governments should be able to buy political advertising on social media sites that advertise in America. There's no reason. They shouldn't. That should be illegal. That's not even a privacy issue. We can regulate that here and we should and people should have to follow the same rules of the road. I think on social media in terms of advertising that they do on other platforms and they ought to have to tell us who they are and who paid for the ads. As you were saying earlier, Suzanne, you know, this is a worry for me because democracy is much more fragile in some ways than other forms of government to this kind of, this particular kind of insidious activity. And that's why we have to be on guard. I am not aware of any good examples of folks taking this on. I've been told that there are some, you may know this, that there are some countries in Eastern Europe where in the evening There are now television channels. dedicated to exposing what lies Russia told that day in their countries, which I can tell you, I would love to have that kind of, not just about the Russians, but other people that lie during that day about our country. But that would be fascinating, you know, and people watch it because they know that it's vital to understanding what's going on in their country. And I wouldn't be surprised to see things like that begin to spring up. Here, one of the reasons why it's so important for us to pass some of this election protection legislation that the majority leader is not allowing us to pass is that Montgomery, Maryland, and that's a fairly wealthy, I just mentioned it because it's here. I mean, they can't take on the Russian internet service by themselves. You think about rural counties in this country and the vulnerability they have to Russian attack and attack by others. They can't do it on their own and they need help. It's a matter of coordination led by Homeland Security and the Justice Department but also with the resources that are necessary to harden Our election, both software and hardware, that's work that we have to take on as well. And I think it would help. By the way, I should say we did a better job in 18 than it should be said, even though we have a president who denies that this actually happened, we did a better job protecting the country in 18 than we did in 16 because of the diligence of the men and women that serve our intelligence agencies. And while the threat is going to evolve and change between now and 2020, I can tell you they are on it in a way that they weren't in 2016, which is a help.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
