  {
    "episode_id": 92914,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92914.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "you. I think that Donald Trump was right to call the question that he called on China. But I think he did it in a completely unproductive way. And when I say I think he was right to call the question, what I mean by that is ever since China, we had a theory as a country that when China entered the WTO in the early 80s, I think it was 1984, I'm not exactly right. What was it? 2001. 2001. A theory that they were going to adopt the rules of the road or the rest of the world, and they never have in terms of trade. And we have to stand up for our own economy, I think. And there are a lot of other countries in the world that feel that way as well. And I think we share equities with countries all over the world, Europe and Asia. I mean, nobody in Asia is interested in being dominated, you know, in a unipolar world by China. They'd much rather be in a bipolar world where the US and China both have an important role to play. So I think that we could lead the entire world, basically, in an effort to push back on China's mercantilist trading practices and say, not what the president is saying, which is we're upset that you're growing, but to say that we know that you're going to grow. We know that as you grow, you present an important opportunity for us as an export market. But as you grow, we're going to expect you to adhere to a set of rules that the rest of us are adhering to. And whether it's state sponsored companies, whether it's the theft of intellectual property, and all the other challenges that we're confronting, including ones related to this, that we're going to be much more aggressive pushing back than we've been. We have put ourselves, I think, in a bad position in this trade war. That's my view, because of the way the president has handled this. And if you don't agree with that, ask yourself who you think has a longer attention span, Beijing or Donald Trump. And that will tell you what you need to know about where we are right now.",
