    "turn_text": "I'd say that's true I'd say that's true and we're living in a moment where we are looking inward when the rest of the world is still relying on us much of the rest world is still relying on us for leadership and my view of that is that that we can repair a lot of this. notwithstanding this four-year interregnum, I think it's going to be much harder to do eight years from now than it is four years from now. Again, that is not a partisan observation. It's just a difference of view. This president has had a very different view about what our engagement in the world should look like compared to Democratic and Republican presidents over the last 70 years. And I think it's fair to say that if there were a different Republican president in the White House, in four years that our approach to these matters would be very different, including the kind of efforts that you're talking about, but also the leadership that are, you know, that our allies, that our Western democracies around the world need us to provide, not to mention countries in Asia as well who are looking for us to provide some leadership there. And we've, in the back end of TPP, essentially abandoned that and we don't have a theory about how to replace that. We've also got a big job to do in our hemisphere, too. And the ambassador was interested to know this, but everywhere I go and every conversation that I have of somebody brings up the refugee crisis at our border. I mean, it's one of the first things that people want to talk about. And the US has an enormous role to play here in terms of not just relieving the immediate refugee crisis that we're facing, but the underlying issues in the northern triangle countries that are causing people to flee for their lives to our border. And we've got a national security interest in doing that. not to mention the humanitarian interests in doing that. And all of this, whether it's the kind of things you're talking about and the stuff that I'm talking about, all of this is going to be much easier to do and much more constructively done if we forge strong alliances around the world with our allies. And we're living in a moment where for some reason we're turning our back on our allies and forging these weird relationships with people that don't have our interests at heart, whether it's North Korea or Russia or anywhere else. Anyway, I got off topic. But that's why I think we need to make a change. And I think it's why we have to not be so careless with our democracy next time. And that's what this book was mostly about, which is let's not be so careless with our democracy.",
